  "label": "TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 10",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 10 — Count solutions of x tan x=1 on [-2π,2π].\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F",
    "G"
  ],
  "correct": "E",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Check division and symmetry",
      "body": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is not a solution, so rewrite the equation as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is an even function.</p>",
      "recap": "Positive solutions have matching negative solutions.",
      "pitfall": "Tangent is undefined at its vertical asymptotes.",
      "pause": "Count on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> first.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l16",
          "reminder": "Check excluded zeros before dividing a trigonometric equation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Select positive tangent branches",
      "body": "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Tangent is positive only on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> inside the positive half of the interval.</p>",
      "recap": "Negative tangent branches cannot intersect a positive reciprocal.",
      "pitfall": "The endpoints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> give tangent zero, not a solution.",
      "pause": "Compare monotonicity on each retained branch.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "Use signs and asymptotes to partition tangent’s domain."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare the graphs on each branch",
      "body": "Sketch the reciprocal and tangent on each retained interval. Compare their directions of travel and their ordering near both ends.",
      "recap": "Endpoint ordering establishes whether a crossing occurs; monotonicity helps decide whether further crossings are possible.",
      "pitfall": "An asymptote separates branches and cannot be counted as an intersection.",
      "pause": "Justify the count on each positive branch, then check symmetry and endpoints.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-root-reciprocal-graphs",
          "reminder": "Endpoint ordering establishes whether a crossing occurs; monotonicity helps decide whether further crossings are possible."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> fails the original equation, division by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> there is exactly one intersection on each of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, because tangent is strictly increasing from 0 to infinity and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is positive and strictly decreasing. There are none on the negative tangent branches.</p><p>The original function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is even, so the two positive roots have two distinct negative partners. The endpoints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> are not roots.</p><p><strong>Answer E: 4 solutions.</strong></p>",
